= Informational inheritance (simple) =

'''Informational inheritance''' means that information or patterns can be passed from one system to another, or preserved when a system changes.

Children inherit genetic instructions from parents. Bacteria can sometimes receive genes from other bacteria. A software agent may restore a saved state, and people can pass knowledge through teaching and language.

These are '''different mechanisms'''. A copied program is not the same thing as a living offspring, and learning a skill is not the same thing as inheriting DNA. They can still be compared by asking what gets transferred, how it is transmitted, and how it changes.

Copying information also does not prove that the new system is the same individual or that it is conscious.

